{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"d477fa3bf144a6d2","name":"card_event_claim_objective","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Germany/OceaniaStorm/events/card_event_claim_objective","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"CLAIM OBJECTIVE","text":"Your units get +1 attack for each kredit spent operating this turn.","flavorText":"During Operation Weserübung in 1940, Germany seized Norway and Denmark before the Allies could threaten the iron ore route from Sweden and use Norwegian ports as naval bases. The plan depended on speed, surprise, and warships carrying troops directly to key cities. Oslo was one of the main objectives, and the heavy cruiser Blücher led the naval group sent up the Oslofjord with 2,000 troops, supported by Lützow, Emden, torpedo boats, minesweepers, and whalers. In the early hours of 9 April, Blücher entered the narrow waters of Drøbak Sound. At Oscarsborg Fortress, Norwegian gunners opened fire after the cruiser was illuminated by searchlights. Two old 280 mm Krupp guns, nicknamed Moses and Aron, hit the ship at close range. Shore-launched torpedoes then struck Blücher, leaving her badly damaged and unable to manoeuvre. Within two hours the cruiser sank, with heavy casualties. The delay gave Norway’s royal family, cabinet, parliament members, and national treasury time to escape Oslo. Germany still occupied Norway, but the failed dash through the fjord changed the opening day of the campaign.","faction":"Germany","type":"order","rarity":"Rare","cost":2,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_claim_objective","title":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_event_claim_objective_title","SourceString":"CLAIM OBJECTIVE","LocalizedString":"CLAIM OBJECTIVE"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::Germany","kredits":2,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_event_claim_objective_text","SourceString":"Your units get +1 attack for each kredit spent operating this turn.","LocalizedString":"Your units get +1 attack for each kredit spent operating this turn."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_event_claim_objective_history","SourceString":"During Operation Weserübung in 1940, Germany seized Norway and Denmark before the Allies could threaten the iron ore route from Sweden and use Norwegian ports as naval bases. The plan depended on speed, surprise, and warships carrying troops directly to key cities. Oslo was one of the main objectives, and the heavy cruiser Blücher led the naval group sent up the Oslofjord with 2,000 troops, supported by Lützow, Emden, torpedo boats, minesweepers, and whalers. In the early hours of 9 April, Blücher entered the narrow waters of Drøbak Sound. At Oscarsborg Fortress, Norwegian gunners opened fire after the cruiser was illuminated by searchlights. Two old 280 mm Krupp guns, nicknamed Moses and Aron, hit the ship at close range. Shore-launched torpedoes then struck Blücher, leaving her badly damaged and unable to manoeuvre. Within two hours the cruiser sank, with heavy casualties. The delay gave Norway’s royal family, cabinet, parliament members, and national treasury time to escape Oslo. Germany still occupied Norway, but the failed dash through the fjord changed the opening day of the campaign.","LocalizedString":"During Operation Weserübung in 1940, Germany seized Norway and Denmark before the Allies could threaten the iron ore route from Sweden and use Norwegian ports as naval bases. The plan depended on speed, surprise, and warships carrying troops directly to key cities. Oslo was one of the main objectives, and the heavy cruiser Blücher led the naval group sent up the Oslofjord with 2,000 troops, supported by Lützow, Emden, torpedo boats, minesweepers, and whalers. In the early hours of 9 April, Blücher entered the narrow waters of Drøbak Sound. At Oscarsborg Fortress, Norwegian gunners opened fire after the cruiser was illuminated by searchlights. Two old 280 mm Krupp guns, nicknamed Moses and Aron, hit the ship at close range. Shore-launched torpedoes then struck Blücher, leaving her badly damaged and unable to manoeuvre. Within two hours the cruiser sank, with heavy casualties. The delay gave Norway’s royal family, cabinet, parliament members, and national treasury time to escape Oslo. Germany still occupied Norway, but the failed dash through the fjord changed the opening day of the campaign."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Rare","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::OceaniaStorm","usedTriggers":["ERegisteredCardFunction::OnOtherCardMoveToFrontline","ERegisteredCardFunction::OnAfterOtherCardAttacks"],"soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'War_UI_Drum_Movement_Field_Snares_3'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/War_UI/Waves/Drum_Movements/War_UI_Drum_Movement_Field_Snares_3.0"},"historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/CLAIM_OBJECTIVE.CLAIM_OBJECTIVE","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_event_claim_objective_photo","SourceString":"German cruiser Blücher after being hit by cannon fire and torpedoes. She sank a short time later. Norway, 1940.","LocalizedString":"German cruiser Blücher after being hit by cannon fire and torpedoes. She sank a short time later. Norway, 1940."},"OnAttackVisualsSoft":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Blueprints/Effects/BP_AttackOrder.BP_AttackOrder_C","SubPathString":""}},"variables":[],"functions":[{"name":"ExecuteUbergraph_card_event_claim_objective","owner":"card_event_claim_objective_C","params":[{"name":"EntryPoint","type":"int32","direction":"in"}],"pure":false,"event":false,"flags":"FUNC_Final | FUNC_UbergraphFunction | FUNC_HasDefaults","locals":[{"name":"CallFunc_IsSideActive_active","type":"bool","direction":"in"},{"name":"Temp_int_Array_Index_Variable","type":"int32","direction":"in"},{"name":"Temp_int_Loop_Counter_Variable","type":"int32","direction":"in"},{"name":"CallFunc_Add_IntInt_ReturnValue","type":"int32","direction":"in"},{"name":"K2Node_